# Dispatch is in source order, so `kept none` answers every none handed to
# `kept` and the arm below it is only ever given a string. The inference has
# to know that: without it, `kept`'s second arm inherits the none it can
# never be given and hands it straight back, so `kept`'s answer reads as one
# that could be a none, and passing that to `shouted` — which has no `none`
# arm — is refused under the per-call exhaustiveness rule, for a none no run
# can produce. `os/read_file!` is the real shape: it answers the builtin's
# none through an `insisted path none` arm, and every caller of its result
# was refused.
fn maybe 0
  none

fn maybe n
  "{n}"

fn kept none
  "nothing"

fn kept s
  s

fn shouted s
  "{s}!"

pub play =
  print (shouted (kept (maybe 0)))
  print (shouted (kept (maybe 7)))
